        "## Part 7: Reflection\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **When would you choose content-based over collaborative filtering, and vice versa?**\n",
        "   - *Content-Based* is ideal when we face a severe item cold-start problem (new items with no ratings but rich metadata) or when user preferences are niche and can be described cleanly by item attributes.\n",
        "   - *Collaborative Filtering* is preferred when we have abundant overlapping user-item interactions and want to make serendipitous, unexpected recommendations that transcend metadata attributes (e.g. recommending a movie of a totally different genre because similar users liked it).\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Why do we mean-centre ratings? Use your Deliberate error 2 result.**\n",
        "   - We mean-centre ratings to remove individual rating biases (such as generous versus harsh raters). Without mean-centering, empty spaces are treated as zero values (highly negative ratings) or raw rating values force missing attributes to dilute similarities, distorting the relative positive/negative orientation of user feedback.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **What is the *filter bubble* problem, and which of the two methods makes it worse?**\n",
        "   - The *filter bubble* occurs when a system keeps recommending highly similar content, isolating the user from new topics.\n",
        "   - *Content-based filtering* makes it worse because it strictly targets items matching existing profile features, leaving no opportunity to explore entirely new niches.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. **Your honest RMSE beat the global-mean baseline by how much? Is that enough to ship? What would you measure online (clicks, watch time) that RMSE cannot show?**\n",
        "   - Honest Item-CF (1.070) beat the Global Mean (1.354) by ~0.284. While mathematically strong, offline RMSE gains do not always translate to user satisfaction.\n",
        "   - Online, we should measure click-through rate (CTR), session conversion rate, and watch time/retention to observe true user engagement.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. **Name one real product that uses a hybrid, and what each half contributes.**\n",
        "   - **Netflix** uses a robust hybrid model.\n",
        "   - The *content-based half* recommends similar shows based on genre, directors, and actors, while the *collaborative filtering half* clusters watch behaviors across millions of globally similar viewers to bubble up personalized trending recommendations.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. **Capstone link (Milestone, Week 10):** could a recommender be part of your capstone? If yes, sketch the user, the item, and the metric. If no, justify.\n",
